# Code generated by Claude
"""
Scene-agnostic unified controller for MuJoCo robots: the launcher.

The control loop lives in src/control_loop.py, the viewer in src/glfw_viewer.py, and the command sources in
src/keyboard_source.py, src/teleop_source.py and src/policy_source.py (commands: src/commands.py, base drive:
src/base_control.py). This file loads the scene, creates the viewer and the sources from the command line and the
CONTROL_GROUPS below, and calls run_loop(). Modes (--mode manual | teleop) and the F2 switch work as before; a policy
is still passed to run() with an "auto" group.

DEFAULT_POLICY = None means manual teleop (keyboard drives the base,
auto groups idle).  To run a policy, set DEFAULT_POLICY to a policy
object, or import run() and pass one in:

    from experiments.combined_controller import run, CONTROL_GROUPS
    run(scene="...", spawn_pos=[...], groups=CONTROL_GROUPS, policy=my_policy)

Each actuator group is either manual or auto, fixed for the session.
Manual types: "drive" (differential-drive, arrow keys) or "joint" (per-joint
up/down keys).  Auto groups use whatever policy is passed to run().
When policy is None, auto groups get zero ctrl.

BASE DRIVE NOTE — velocity control, not wheel torques:
    The Tracer base is driven by setting the free joint's qvel directly
    rather than torquing the wheel motor actuators.  This is necessary
    because MuJoCo mesh-on-plane wheel contacts don't roll: mesh vertices
    are fixed on the surface, so instead of tracking smoothly like a real
    wheel, they slide on whichever vertices happen to be nearest the
    floor.  Under the ~70 kg combined robot, the contacts either absorb
    all motor torque (at low gear) or let the wheels spin freely at
    thousands of rad/s with no traction (at high gear).

    Velocity control also better matches the real Tracer's interface:
    the physical robot accepts cmd_vel (linear/angular velocity) over
    ROS2, and its onboard controller handles wheel-level conversion
    internally.  Policies trained against velocity-controlled sim will
    transfer more cleanly than policies that learned to fight mesh
    friction artifacts.

    To restore torque-based wheel control, replace the mesh wheel geoms
    in combined_robot.xml with cylinder primitives (cylinder-on-plane
    contacts roll correctly in MuJoCo) and switch this back to the
    "drive_torque" handler.

RATES NOTE:
    Physics runs at robot_specs.PHYSICS_HZ (750 Hz, the OpenArm ros2_control
    loop); the arm and finger PD runs inside MuJoCo (position actuators, see
    src/arm_actuators.py), so it is evaluated on every physics step;
    the simulated cameras tick at CAMERA_HZ (30 Hz, every 25 physics
    steps). By default ONE camera is rendered per tick, taking turns, so each
    updates at CAMERA_HZ / 3 = 10 Hz. That take-turns mode is a VM
    PERFORMANCE setting (a 3-camera render dominates the frame time on the
    VM's virtual GPU), not a model of the real cameras. For recording demos
    run with CC_ROUNDROBIN=0 to render all three cameras on every tick, so
    each updates at the full CAMERA_HZ in sim time (the viewer then runs
    slower than real time on the VM).
    The viewer redraws at VIEWER_HZ (60, 12 physics steps per frame).
    Keys, policy and teleop targets are read once per viewer frame and held
    between frames and written to data.ctrl as joint angles; the MuJoCo position
    servos track them from the LIVE state on every physics step. Teleop targets are not clocked at
    hardware solver rates because the 750 Hz loop is the hardware ceiling.

CAMERAS NOTE:
    robot_specs.CAMERA_SPECS (eagle + two wrist cams, Orbbec placeholders)
    are injected into the model at load time and rendered offscreen by
    src.cameras.CameraRig into rig.images. When SHOW_CAMERA_INSETS is on they
    are drawn at half size along the bottom of the viewer. Press F3 to save
    the latest frames as PNGs to data/camera_snapshots/.

TELEOP NOTE:
    --mode teleop starts with the arms in MANUAL (each joint has its own
    key pair: left arm 1-8 up / Q-I down, right arm A-K up / Z-, down; the last
    column is the gripper) and the base on the arrow keys in every state.
    F2 switches the
    arms to the webcam operator (and back); manual keys and teleop write the
    same per-joint targets, so nothing jumps. The grippers stay on the keys
    in both states (the teleop solver only outputs arm joints). In TELEOP, if the operator
    leaves view the arms hold their last pose; they follow again when the
    engage gesture is made. The webcam feed (with pose overlay) is drawn as a
    fourth inset beside the simulated cameras; there is no second window.
    CC_FAKE_TELEOP=1 swaps in a scripted operator for testing without a webcam.

RECORDING NOTE:
    F6 (or the --record argument, which starts it on the first frame) starts / stops recording the viewer image to data/recordings/viewer_<time>.mp4
    and writes viewer_<time>_joints.csv beside it: one row per video frame, flushed as
    it goes, with sim time, the base pose and every arm joint / finger as <joint>_pos
    (actual) and <joint>_target (PD target). The MP4 frame rate is sim time (physics
    rate / physics steps per viewer frame), so playback runs at real simulated speed.
    Use CC_ROUNDROBIN=0 for full-rate camera insets in the video.

Usage:
    python experiments/combined_controller.py
    python experiments/combined_controller.py --scene models/scenes/other.xml
    python experiments/combined_controller.py --spawn -1 3.5 0.22
    python experiments/combined_controller.py --mode teleop --camera 0
"""

